@page "/chart"

<CatHead Title="Charts" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    Lines, areas, columns, bars, pies, donuts and scatters, drawn in SVG from a table the app writes. The
    table is the data and the text alternative at once; the library draws the chart beside it. The drawing
    is the one thing it paints &mdash; the legend, the tooltip, the wait, the empty state and every word
    around the chart are the app's own markup with the classes on this page.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-bar-chart-2-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Nothing goes in the host page</span>
        <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> finds every <code>[data-chart]</code> and imports the drawing the first time one
        comes within a screen of the viewport, so a page without a chart never downloads it. There is no engine
        behind it and no C# service: a component renders the table, and the chart follows. Every attribute,
        field, key and member is in <code>docs/charts.md</code>. For a handful of bars beside a number, the
        <a href="/bars">bars and meters</a> and <a href="/sparkline">sparklines</a> need no script at all.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Line" Title="A line from a table" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The least a chart needs: a <code>.chart</code> with <code>data-chart</code>, an <code>id</code> and an
        <code>aria-label</code>; its data as a <code>.chart-data</code> table; a <code>.chart-plot</code> to draw
        into; and a <code>.chart-wait</code> covering it until the drawing is there. The header row names the
        series, one column each; every body row is a category, its <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> the label. An empty
        cell is a gap in the line, not a zero.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The table is the data and the text alternative.</strong> It is visually hidden and a screen reader
        reads it as a table; with scripting off, it is shown in place of the drawing. A cell's number is its
        <code>data-value</code>, else its text read as a plain number, and its text is what the tooltip and the
        announcement say &mdash; so a value is written the way the app writes a number. Point at the chart to see
        a value; with no tooltip of the app's, the library's own hover-hint bubble says the category, the series
        and the value.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Columns" Title="Columns, a tooltip and a legend that switches" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart="column"</code> sets each category's series side by side. The tooltip is the app's
        <code>.chart-tip</code>, written once inside the chart and filled per point: every
        <code>[data-chart-field]</code> gets that field as text, and a
        <code>&lt;template data-chart-tip-row&gt;</code> makes it say the whole category &mdash; a
        <code>.chart-tip-row</code> per series, its <code>.chart-tip-swatch</code> in the series' colour and its
        <code>.chart-tip-value</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        A <code>button.chart-key</code> with <code>data-chart-toggle</code> and <code>aria-pressed</code> hides and
        shows its series, and the value axis fits what is left. It drives the chart in its
        <code>[data-chart-frame]</code>, or the one <code>data-chart-for</code> names. <code>data-chart-format</code>
        is the <code>Intl.NumberFormat</code> options the axis writes its numbers with &mdash; compact here &mdash;
        in the page's language.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Stacked" Title="Stacked, in semantic tones" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart-stack</code> stacks a column, a bar or an area chart. <code>data-tone</code> on a header
        cell names a series' colour: <code>1</code>&ndash;<code>6</code> for series that are merely different, and
        <code>go</code>, <code>warn</code> and <code>danger</code> only where the series <em>means</em> that &mdash;
        a red series says &ldquo;danger&rdquo; whether or not the data does. <code>{percent}</code> in the
        announcement is a value's share of its category.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Area" Title="Areas" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart="area"</code> draws a line over its filled area; stacked, the layers add up to the
        whole, and a missing value adds nothing to the layer below.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Bars" Title="Bars, for long names" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart="bar"</code> lays the columns down, so the category names run down the side and have the
        room a column chart's would not. With a single series, <code>data-tone</code> on a row colours that one
        category &mdash; the zone below its target here &mdash; and the chart's <code>aria-label</code> says why.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Donut" Title="Pies and donuts" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart="pie"</code> and <code>"donut"</code> draw the first series, a slice per category from
        twelve o'clock, taking the series colours in order unless a row names its tone. <code>.chart-total</code>
        puts the app's figure in a donut's hole: a <code>.chart-total-value</code> and a
        <code>.chart-total-label</code>. In a pie a legend key's <code>data-chart-toggle</code> names a category,
        and the arrow keys walk the slices.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Scatter" Title="Scatter" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-chart="scatter"</code> puts each value at its row's own number across &mdash; the row
        heading's <code>data-value</code> or text &mdash; and the value up. <code>data-chart-min="auto"</code> lets
        a line or a scatter start near its lowest value; everywhere else the value axis takes in zero, because a
        bar that does not start at zero misstates its value. <code>data-chart-min</code> and
        <code>data-chart-max</code> fix either end.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Shapes" Title="A shape for every series" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>A series is told apart by its shape as well as its colour.</strong> The closest series colours
        rely on it under colour-vision deficiency, so every series has its own marker &mdash; circle, square,
        diamond, triangle, the triangle turned down, a cross &mdash; and the legend names it with the
        <code>.chart-swatch</code> of the same shape: <code>--square</code>, <code>--diamond</code>,
        <code>--triangle</code>, <code>--triangle-down</code>, <code>--cross</code>. <code>data-shape</code> on a
        header cell picks another. A filled mark's key is <code>--block</code>, a line's <code>--line</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every mark is an SVG element wearing the <code>.series-*</code> class of its tone, so the browser paints it
        the theme's colour in every variant, colour vision, contrast setting and under forced colours, and
        repaints it with no script when any of them changes. There are no chart colour tokens: a chart's colours
        are the series every other figure uses.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.FromJson" Title="Data as JSON" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        For a series too long to read as a table, the data goes in <code>data-chart-json</code> on the chart's
        element &mdash; an attribute, so a component binds it and a render redraws. A value given as a bare
        number is said in the chart's format. With no table there is no text alternative but the chart's own
        name, so the <code>aria-label</code> says what the data shows.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Sizes" Title="Sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.chart</code> is 16rem tall, <code>.chart--sm</code> 10rem and <code>.chart--lg</code> 24rem;
        <code>--chart-height</code> sets any other, and <code>.chart--fill</code> takes its container's height.
        The width is always the container's, and the drawing follows it as the container resizes.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.States" Title="Loading, empty and failed" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The script writes <code>data-chart-state</code> &mdash; <code>ready</code>, or <code>empty</code> when the
        data holds no value &mdash; and the app's <code>.chart-wait</code> covers the plot until it does.
        <code>.chart-empty</code> on an <code>.empty-state</code> shows when the data is empty. Loading and failing
        are the app's to say, because only the app knows: <code>aria-busy="true"</code> while it fetches brings
        the wait back, and <code>data-chart-error</code> shows <code>.chart-empty--error</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.Keyboard" Title="The keyboard and the screen reader" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>The chart is one stop in the tab order.</strong> Reaching it puts the reader on the first point;
        <kbd>&larr;</kbd> <kbd>&rarr;</kbd> move between categories as they are drawn, <kbd>&uarr;</kbd>
        <kbd>&darr;</kbd> between series, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> to the ends, <kbd>Enter</kbd> or
        <kbd>Space</kbd> chooses the point and <kbd>Escape</kbd> lets go. A bar chart lies down, so there the two
        pairs of arrows swap. The point is ringed and the tooltip shows it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every move is said through the app's <code>data-chart-live</code> region, in the app's words:
        <code>data-chart-announce</code> with <code>{category}</code>, <code>{series}</code>, <code>{value}</code>,
        <code>{percent}</code>, <code>{index}</code> and <code>{count}</code>, and
        <code>data-chart-announce-select</code> when a point is chosen. The chart gets
        <code>role="application"</code> and <code>tabindex="0"</code> unless the app wrote its own; its name and
        <code>aria-roledescription</code> are the app's.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ChartSelect" Title="A chosen point in Blazor" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>@@onsedna-chart-select</code> receives a <code>SednaChartEventArgs</code>
        &mdash; the chart, the series, the category, the value as a number and as the app wrote it &mdash; when a
        point is clicked, tapped, or chosen with <kbd>Enter</kbd>. What it means is the app's. Adding a week
        re-renders the table, and the chart draws it: there is nothing to call.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chart.RightToLeft" Title="Right to left" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        In a right-to-left page the categories run from the right, the value axis's numbers sit on the right and
        a bar grows from the right; <kbd>&rarr;</kbd> still moves to the point drawn to the right. The axis writes
        its numbers in the page's language. The value axis still rises upwards.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="drawing">The drawing's classes <CatAnchor Id="drawing" /></h2>
<p>
    The script draws one <code>.chart-svg</code> into the plot and writes these classes on what is inside it:
    <code>.chart-grid</code> and <code>.chart-axis</code> for the gridlines and the zero line,
    <code>.chart-tick</code> and <code>.chart-category</code> for the numbers and the labels,
    <code>.chart-mark</code> on every mark with <code>.chart-line</code>, <code>.chart-area</code>,
    <code>.chart-bar</code>, <code>.chart-marker</code> or <code>.chart-slice</code>, and for the point the reader
    is on <code>.chart-ring</code>, <code>.chart-marker--active</code>, <code>.chart-guide</code> through a line
    and <code>.chart-band</code> behind a column. An app never writes one, and may restyle one from its own
    stylesheet like any class. The marks are grouped in <code>.chart-marks</code>, which fades in on the first
    drawing; under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> it is shown whole at once.
</p>
